<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/foundational/ecosystems-energy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An ecosystem is a community of organisms plus the physical world they inhabit — the biotic
    (plants, animals, fungi, microbes) and the abiotic (sunlight, water, soil, temperature)
    bound into one working system. Two rules govern it. <em>Matter cycles</em>: carbon, nitrogen
    and water pass from soil to plant to animal and back, endlessly. <em>Energy flows</em>: it
    enters once, as sunlight, and leaks away at every step as heat, never to return. An
    ecosystem is therefore an energy pipeline with terrible efficiency — which turns out to
    explain most of its shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sunlight hits the <strong>producers</strong> — plants, algae, some bacteria — which convert
    roughly 1–2% of it into biomass. That energy then flows upward through
    <strong>trophic levels</strong> (herbivores, then predators, then top predators), and at
    every transfer about <strong>90% is lost</strong>: burned in movement and body heat, voided
    in excretion, or locked in indigestible parts like cellulose and bone, with the residue
    passed to decomposers. The "ten-percent rule" is the rule of thumb that falls out —
    formalised by Raymond Lindeman in 1942 in a study of Silver Springs, Florida, which
    measured the flow in calories directly.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why there are no wolf-eating dragons — energy at each level, on the ten-percent rule" widget="plotter" config={tenPctCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve honestly: by the fourth consumer level, a thousand units of plant energy have
    dwindled to 0.1. That is why food chains rarely run past four or five links, why big
    predators need huge territories (a tiger's range is hundreds of square kilometres — it is
    the far end of the pipeline), and why apex carnivores are always the first to vanish when
    habitat shrinks.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring the flow: from gross to net productivity</h2>
  <p>
    Ecologists keep two ledgers at the pipeline's base. <strong>Gross primary productivity</strong>
    (GPP) is the total energy plants fix by photosynthesis; <strong>net primary
    productivity</strong> (NPP) is what remains after the plants pay their own respiration —
    the actual surplus available to everything else. NPP is the single most important number in
    ecosystem ecology, and it varies by two orders of magnitude across the planet: tropical
    rainforest fixes around 2,200 grams of biomass per square metre per year, desert under 100,
    open ocean a thin 125 spread over three quarters of the planet. The chart below compares
    the classic values:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="net primary productivity by biome — where the planetary surplus is actually made" widget="chart" config={nppCfg} />

  <p>
    The trophic handoffs have now been measured, not just modelled. Odum's Silver Springs
    numbers: producers fixed 20,810 kcal per m² per year; herbivores received 3,368 (about
    16% — a generous pipeline); small carnivores 383; top carnivores just 21. Decomposers
    processed 5,060 — more than the herbivores — a reminder that the "invisible" end of the
    pipeline handles the bulk of the flow. Pyramids pile up everywhere as a consequence: of
    energy, of standing biomass, and usually of numbers — a meadow supports millions of
    insects, dozens of birds, one kestrel. (The one famous inversion: in open ocean, the
    phytoplankton biomass at any instant is smaller than the krill eating it, because
    phytoplankton divide so fast they are a tiny reservoir with a huge throughput — energy
    pyramids still stand; only the snapshot biomass topples.)
  </p>

  <h2>Forest ecosystems: three designs for one job</h2>
  <p>
    Forests are the planet's great energy collectors, but the three main kinds run on opposite
    strategies. <strong>Tropical rainforest</strong> is a skyscraper: light is so abundant that
    the canopy layers itself — emergents, canopy, understorey, dark floor — and over half of
    all land species live in it. Its counterintuitive secret is <em>poor soil</em>: nutrients
    cycle so fast that almost nothing is stored in the ground; the wealth is in the living
    biomass, which is why cleared rainforest turns to hardpan within a few seasons and why the
    Amazon's fertility is a myth of the canopy, not the dirt. <strong>Temperate deciduous
    forest</strong> trades the endless growing season for winter dormancy — dropping leaves
    annually, building rich brown soils from their compost, supporting fewer species but larger
    standing biomass per species. <strong>Boreal forest</strong> (taiga) runs cold and slow:
    evergreen needles (a running start each short summer), acidic litter, and soil fungi doing
    decomposition work that cold bacteria cannot — the slowest, most carbon-hoarding of the
    three, which is why boreal peat and permafrost are such outsized carbon stores. All three
    matter to the carbon ledger: forests hold roughly 450–650 GtC in biomass and soils, and
    deforestation releases about 1–1.5 GtC a year — a tenth of the fossil problem, in one
    land-use decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Nutrient cycles and the decomposer economy</h2>
  <p>
    The pipeline's leaky inefficiency only works because nothing material is lost. Decomposers
    — bacteria and fungi, plus detritivores like earthworms and woodlice — are the recycling
    plant: they break dead matter into soluble molecules that roots reabsorb. Without them the
    world would drown in undecayed cellulose and the nutrient supply would jam within years.
    Two cycles deserve special mention. The <strong>nitrogen cycle</strong> turns atmospheric
    N₂ — 78% of the air, yet unusable by plants — into usable nitrate via nitrogen-fixing
    bacteria (free-living ones in soil, and <em>Rhizobium</em> in the root nodules of beans and
    clover, the reason farmers rotate legumes); other bacteria then return it to N₂. The
    <strong>carbon cycle</strong> couples every meal to the atmosphere, and mycorrhizal fungi
    — trading minerals to tree roots for sugar — knit the whole forest floor into one trading
    network covering most of the land surface.
  </p>

  <h2>Biodiversity and how to keep it</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Biodiversity</strong> is measured on two axes: species richness (how many kinds)
    and evenness (how fairly individuals are spread among them — a forest of 10 species in
    equal numbers is more diverse than one where 95% belong to a single species). Richness
    climbs predictably from poles to tropics and from small to large areas, and it is
    measurable in the field — this chapter's fieldwork section shows how. Why protect it?
    Three honest answers: ecosystems with more species tend to be more productive and more
    stable (insurance against shocks); biodiversity is our pharmacy and crop gene pool; and
    the loss is permanent — extinction has no undo.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not all species are equal in influence. <strong>Keystone species</strong> punch far above
    their energy budget: remove wolves from Yellowstone (as happened by 1926) and elk
    overgraze riverbanks, collapsing willows, beavers and songbird habitat; reintroduce them
    in 1995 and the rivers literally change course-ward as vegetation returns — a
    <em>trophic cascade</em>. Sea otters hunting sea urchins protect kelp forests; a single
    starfish species structures a whole intertidal zone (Paine's classic experiment).
    Management, therefore, is not just "more of everything" but knowing which interactions
    hold the system together. The standard toolkit: protected areas (parks and reserves,
    sized with population viability in mind), corridors reconnecting fragmented habitat,
    species-specific programmes (captive breeding, CITES trade bans for elephants and
    rhinos), and community-based use — ecotourism and sustainable harvest, on the logic that
    a forest worth money standing survives better than one worth money felled. Conservation
    priorities cluster in <strong>biodiversity hotspots</strong> — regions with huge numbers
    of endemic species that have already lost most of their original habitat (Madagascar, the
    tropical Andes, Southeast Asia) — where a conservation pound buys the most extinctions
    prevented.
  </p>

  <h2>Fieldwork: how ecologists actually count</h2>
  <p>
    Ecology is measurable, and the measurement tools are simple enough to use yourself. A
    <strong>quadrat</strong> — a square frame (often 1 m²) tossed at random positions — lets
    you estimate abundance: count individuals per quadrat and scale up by area, or record
    percentage cover for plants too numerous to count. <strong>Transects</strong> sample along
    a line instead, which is how you capture change across a gradient — a seashore zonation
    from barnacles to bladderwrack, or a woodland edge into deep shade. Mobile animals need
    the <strong>mark–recapture</strong> method: catch a sample, mark and release them, catch
    again. If you marked 40 beetles and your second catch of 30 contains 10 marked, then
    marked/unmarked in the second sample mirrors the whole population: 10/30 = 40/N, so
    N ≈ 120. (The catch: marked animals must mix back in, and marks must not change behaviour —
    beetles painted bright red get eaten selectively, and the estimate collapses.) Wrap every
    estimate in repetition — many quadrats, not one — and an honest error range, and you are
    doing the same science the textbooks cite.
  </p>

  <h2>Succession: ecosystems have a growth arc</h2>
  <p>
    Left alone, an ecosystem builds itself in a predictable sequence called
    <strong>succession</strong>. On bare rock — after a lava flow or a retreating glacier —
    the first arrivals are <em>pioneer species</em>: lichens, which need no soil because they
    are a fungus–alga partnership that dissolves rock directly. Their dead matter, with
    windblown dust, makes the first thin soil; mosses take over; then grasses, then shrubs,
    then fast-growing trees, each stage trapping more water and organic matter until a
    <em>climax community</em> — the tall forest the local climate allows — stabilises the
    system. After a disturbance that leaves soil intact (a fire, a cleared field), the same
    arc runs from grass instead of lichen, much faster: <em>secondary</em> succession, the
    reason an abandoned field becomes woodland within decades. The energy logic ties back to
    the pyramid: each successional stage captures more of the incoming sunlight (higher NPP)
    and stores more of it in standing biomass — a forest is the most complete answer a
    landscape can give to the question of what to do with a photon.
  </p>

  <h2>The pyramid on your plate</h2>
  <p>
    The ten-percent rule scales to dinner. Livestock are mid-pipeline converters: a beef
    animal returns roughly a tenth of the plant energy it eats as meat (feed-conversion
    ratios run around 10 kg of feed per 1 kg of beef; pork ~4:1, chicken ~2:1). The
    consequence is a land-use multiplier: producing the same protein from plants
    (pulses, grains, soy) typically needs 5–15 times less land and a fraction of the water,
    because it eats at the pipeline's base instead of one level up. This arithmetic — not
    ethics alone — is behind the environmental-science consensus that plant-rich diets are
    among the largest single levers on both land use and agricultural emissions. It is the
    same figure-1 curve, run in agricultural quantities: every trophic level you climb costs
    a factor of ten, and civilisation currently eats near the top.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The ten-percent rule is a pedagogical honest lie: measured transfer efficiencies run from
    5% to 20% (ectotherms — cold-blooded animals — transfer better than birds and mammals,
    which burn half their intake as heat), and real ecosystems are <em>food webs</em>, not
    chains: omnivory, detrital pathways and microbial loops criss-cross the pyramid, which is
    why ecosystem modelling moved from Lindeman's neat diagrams to full energy-flow matrices.
    Lindeman himself (who died at 27) had his landmark paper rejected as "interesting but
    prematurely published" — the referees thought quantifying ecology was a generation too
    early; it instead founded an entire discipline. The modern frontier pushes in two
    directions: upward into <em>ecosystem services</em> — the Millennium Ecosystem Assessment's
    four-way split (provisioning, regulating, cultural, supporting) now underlies how the
    world prices pollination, flood control and carbon storage — and downward into the soil
    microbiome, where the majority of species are unnamed and the deepest carbon stores live.
    The pipeline metaphor holds; what keeps surprising us is how much of the flow runs through
    pipes nobody has mapped.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lindeman, Ecology (1942); Odum, "Trophic Structure and Productivity of Silver Springs" (1957); Whittaker & Likens, <em>Communities and Ecosystems</em> (1975); Millennium Ecosystem Assessment (2005); US National Park Service (Yellowstone wolf reintroduction).</p>
</TopicPage>
